export anchor Thesis:
    introduction:
        Behaviour and general concepts are easy to describe in prose. But my
        goodness, when you start trying to describe an image, a user interface,
        a button, a layout, etc., AI rapidly looses grasp of whatever dimension
        it calls reality.

        And that's not unresonable in the slightest that it struggles; models
        are trained on many kinds of input, but your or my definition of a
        "pretty picture" isn't one of them. What's far more reasonable is to
        accept the assumption that most of the internet now is pretty, and that
        HTML and CSS are the core building blocks -- in fact the only actualy
        _blocks_- of how all of it all comes together. And further that the
        infinitely expanding universe of training data that is the Internet is
        exactly why models can turn HTML/CSS into a rather precise visual
        outcome. Aestheticaly pleasing deterministic trainging data -- wow.

        So that's incredible. And HTML/CSS is something that most software
        developers know to some extent. And thanks to tools like Figma,
        designers understand the mechanisms even if they've never touched a line
        of code. But in a world where authorship itself is far less valuable
        than creative intent, how do we capture the disparate skillsets involved
        in expressing and implementing that intent into a single, robust source?

        Emul is a proposed solution to that question. It's a language that
        provides limited approximation of the design model that HTML/CSS
        provides, while simplifying the implementation and authorship details
        such that legacy and browser quirks are not within scope.  The goal is
        to allow creating a robust visual implementation; we want a
        model-friendly visual implementation *and* a pretty that you can view.

    example:
        ```emul
        \\\
        component DefaultButton:
            htmlTag: button
            display: flex
            alignItems: center
            borderRadius: 6px
            backgroundColor: #e4e4e7

        component MyButton extends DefaultButton:
            padding: 8px 12px
            color: #dc2626

        component SecondaryButton extends MyButton:
            backgroundColor: #ffffff
            border: 1px solid #dc2626

        component MyLayout:
            display: flex
            flexDirection: row
            gap: 16px
            padding: 64px

        composition MyComposition {
            MyLayout {
                SecondaryButton { "Click Me" }
                MyButton { "Click Me Again", title: The second button }
            }
        }
        \\\
        ```
